1
00:00:12,690 --> 00:00:17,076
Lay potato chips. Bet you can't eat just one.

2
00:00:17,076 --> 00:00:19,096
A slogan that's become legendary.

3
00:00:19,096 --> 00:00:22,210
Tied to one of the most irresistible snacks on the planet.

4
00:00:22,210 --> 00:00:24,050
Lays potato chips.

5
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:27,469
And honestly, once you open the bag, you'll get it.

6
00:00:27,469 --> 00:00:30,983
These thin, golden crisp slices aren't just a snack.

7
00:00:30,983 --> 00:00:33,689
They've become part of daily routines for millions.

8
00:00:33,689 --> 00:00:42,237
And yeah, even a full-blown addiction for some.

9
00:00:42,237 --> 00:00:49,021
In this video, we're taking you inside the Frito- Lay factory to uncover how these iconic bags of Lays potato chips are made.

10
00:00:49,021 --> 00:00:53,328
Trust us, it's a crunchy journey you won't want to miss.

11
00:00:53,328 --> 00:00:57,181
This is one of PepsiCo's largest and most advanced factories in Europe.

12
00:00:57,181 --> 00:00:59,887
And it's where millions of bags of Lays are made.

13
00:00:59,887 --> 00:01:02,193
The same ones we casually snack on every day.

14
00:01:02,193 --> 00:01:15,002
With a jaw-dropping output of around four tons of potato chips per hour, this facility processes over 750 tons of raw potatoes daily, running more than four production lines non-stop to keep the crispy goodness coming.

15
00:01:15,002 --> 00:01:22,391
PepsiCo is now one of the world's biggest potato buyers, sourcing up to 240,000 tons annually.

16
00:01:22,391 --> 00:01:27,022
And of course, only the highest quality spuds make the cut to become Lays.

17
00:01:27,022 --> 00:01:32,531
The journey begins in vast potato fields where harvest season is in full swing.

18
00:01:32,531 --> 00:01:37,860
Since potatoes grow underground, farmers use a specialized digging machine.

19
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:45,499
This powerful rig can reach up to 10 in deep, cleanly lifting entire plants from the soil, roots, rocks, dirt, and all.

20
00:01:45,499 --> 00:01:53,329
Everything gets loaded onto a vibrating conveyor screen that shakes off excess soil while sorting through up to 40,000 potatoes per minute.

21
00:01:53,329 --> 00:01:58,731
From there, the potatoes are transferred directly into trucks driving alongside the harvester.

22
00:01:58,731 --> 00:02:03,928
This synchronized setup keeps the operation smooth, steady, and interruption-free.

23
00:02:03,928 --> 00:02:12,937
Once full, the trucks head straight to a nearby storage hub, a massive facility purpose-built to handle hundreds of tons of fresh potatoes every single day.

24
00:02:12,937 --> 00:02:17,070
Next, the potatoes are funneled into an optical sorder.

25
00:02:17,070 --> 00:02:24,276
Massive machines running at full throttle, all with one critical mission, finding the perfect spuds.

26
00:02:24,276 --> 00:02:29,685
Fresh, firm potatoes ride along a conveyor belt, passing through a sophisticated scanning system.

27
00:02:29,685 --> 00:02:37,318
Here, high precision sensors inspect each potato in detail, analyzing its size, shape, and color with incredible accuracy.

28
00:02:37,318 --> 00:02:45,162
Meanwhile, nimble pneumatic robotic arms spring into action, swiftly, removing clumps of dirt that got mixed in with the potatoes.

29
00:02:45,162 --> 00:02:49,195
They move so fast it's nearly impossible to follow with the naked eye.

30
00:02:49,195 --> 00:02:57,455
Finally, any potatoes that don't make the cut. Those that are bruised, diseased, or just too small are instantly ejected from the line.

31
00:02:57,455 --> 00:03:01,239
Only the top tier spuds move on to cold storage.

32
00:03:01,239 --> 00:03:08,885
Freshly pulled from the earth and still dusted with soil, the potatoes are gently placed onto a conveyor belt and moved into massive wooden crates.

33
00:03:08,885 --> 00:03:16,526
From there, they continue their journey into a cold storage facility with enough room to hold a staggering 40 million potatoes at once.

34
00:03:16,526 --> 00:03:22,793
Here, the Spuds rest anywhere from a few weeks to several months, depending on production schedules.

35
00:03:22,793 --> 00:03:31,665
[Wash] And when the&nbsp;Frito- Lay&nbsp;factory calls for a new batch, forklifts get to work, hauling the crates out of storage and onto a conveyor leading straight to the washing station.

36
00:03:31,665 --> 00:03:41,879
This is the first step in getting the potatoes camera ready, mainly to scrub off any dirt and debris because Frito-Lay is paying for potatoes, not the soil they came with.

37
00:03:41,879 --> 00:03:49,903
Next up, a team of workers performs a final manual inspection, weeding out any stragglers that don't meet quality standards.

38
00:03:49,903 --> 00:03:56,017
Clean, shiny, and prepped for glory. The potatoes roll onto the belt one last time.

39
00:03:56,017 --> 00:03:59,206
This time into the waiting containers at the back of a truck.

40
00:03:59,206 --> 00:04:02,365
And from here, things speed up fast.

41
00:04:02,365 --> 00:04:08,825
In less than 24 hours, these fresh spuds will arrive at the plant and become Lays Classic.

42
00:04:08,825 --> 00:04:18,818
When a truckload of potatoes arrives at the factory gate, the entire vehicle is lifted onto a massive hydraulic platform, then slowly tilted forward at about a 30° angle.

43
00:04:18,818 --> 00:04:26,701
And then, like a tidal wave of tubers, thousands of potatoes come tumbling down onto the conveyor belt below.

44
00:04:26,701 --> 00:04:34,487
From there, the potatoes are routed to the incoming quality control area, where random samples are pulled for starch content testing.

45
00:04:34,487 --> 00:04:41,716
Even though the spuds were already washed once back at the farm, the entire load is sent through an industrial washing system all over again.

46
00:04:41,716 --> 00:04:46,930
It's not just about cleanliness, it's also about protecting the factory's precision equipment.

47
00:04:46,930 --> 00:04:54,030
Just one tiny speck of leftover dirt could scratch a blade or mess with the peeling machines and other delicate systems downstream.

48
00:04:54,030 --> 00:05:03,374
This high-powered rinse uses a combination of intense water jets, industrial brushes, and a swirling water vortex to make sure each potato comes out spotless.

49
00:05:03,374 --> 00:05:08,446
This system is no slouch. It can wash up to 6 million potatoes a day.

50
00:05:08,446 --> 00:05:17,036
[Peeler] Next, the potatoes enter one of six large-scale peelers, each lined with a rough, abrasive material on the inside.

51
00:05:17,036 --> 00:05:24,515
As the machine spins, the potatoes tumble and roll, rubbing against the gritty surface, which gently scrapes off the skin.

52
00:05:24,515 --> 00:05:31,189
At the same time, high pressure water jets spray the spinning spuds, washing away the loosened peels.

53
00:05:31,189 --> 00:05:37,064
Once peeled, the potatoes pass through an advanced camera system that scans each one in real time.

54
00:05:37,064 --> 00:05:42,065
In just a split second, these cameras can detect signs of rot or visual defects.

55
00:05:42,065 --> 00:05:49,906
And if something's off, a mechanical finger shoots a targeted puff of air to eject the faulty potato from the stream for other uses.

56
00:05:49,906 --> 00:05:53,606
The good ones move on along a specialized conveyor system.

57
00:05:53,606 --> 00:05:57,410
[Size Sorter] Here, an automated sorter separates the crop by size.

58
00:05:57,410 --> 00:06:01,416
Only standard-sized potatoes fall through the slots for further processing.

59
00:06:01,416 --> 00:06:09,545
While oversized ones are diverted to a cutting station where they're halved before continuing down the line to make sure nothing slips through the cracks.

60
00:06:09,545 --> 00:06:13,475
Workers are stationed along the conveyor for one last round of human inspection.&nbsp;

61
00:06:13,475 --> 00:06:20,189
They keep a sharp eye out for damaged, bruised, or partially peeled potatoes that may have escaped the machines.&nbsp;

62
00:06:20,189 --> 00:06:25,766
If they spot any spuds that are still too large, they'll slice them in half right there on the spot.

63
00:06:25,766 --> 00:06:30,781
[Slice] Next, the potatoes are loaded onto a conveyor and dropped into a rotating drum slicer.

64
00:06:30,781 --> 00:06:38,656
Inside, powerful centrifugal force flings each potato outward, pressing it against razor-sharp blades waiting at the perimeter.

65
00:06:38,656 --> 00:06:50,996
In a flash, each spud is precisely sliced into 35 uniform chips, each one exactly 0.06 inches thick, the perfect cut for a Lay's chip before it hits the fryer.

66
00:06:50,996 --> 00:06:56,700
[Slice检查] Right after slicing, quality control technicians continuously check the thickness of the chips.

67
00:06:56,700 --> 00:07:00,083
Even the slightest variation can ruin the texture.

68
00:07:00,083 --> 00:07:07,112
Too thick and the chip turns out flat and heavy. Too thin and it curls or folds unevenly.

69
00:07:07,112 --> 00:07:10,998
The goal: a perfectly even saddle-shaped curve.

70
00:07:10,998 --> 00:07:13,380
That signature Lay's look.

71
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:17,484
Depending on the product line, the slicing blades are switched out.

72
00:07:17,484 --> 00:07:21,965
Lay's Classic uses straight blades for that smooth, traditional cut.

73
00:07:21,965 --> 00:07:31,490
Lay's Wavy or Ruffles, on the other hand, use serrated blades to create bold rippled edges that not only look great, but also add an extra crunch.

74
00:07:31,490 --> 00:07:37,857
[Rinse] Once sliced, the potato slices drop into a fast-moving stream of water that carries them straight to the washing station.

75
00:07:37,857 --> 00:07:42,674
There they tumble through a spinning drum and go through multiple rinse cycles.

76
00:07:42,674 --> 00:07:49,553
First a soak, then a high-pressure spray from above, followed by a second water bath and another round of jets.

77
00:07:49,553 --> 00:07:52,336
All of this is designed to wash off surface starch.

78
00:07:52,336 --> 00:07:56,990
If left behind, the starch would cause the chips to stick together during frying.

79
00:07:56,990 --> 00:08:00,822
[Fryer] Next, the potato slices are dried and transferred to the fryer.

80
00:08:00,822 --> 00:08:03,607
They're cooked in a hot blend of sunflower oil.

81
00:08:03,607 --> 00:08:13,265
The thin slices are dropped into a massive continuous fryer over 40 feet long where they're fully submerged in a bubbling mix of sunflower, canola, and vegetable oils.

82
00:08:13,265 --> 00:08:19,566
The temperature hovers around 350°F, and the chips fry for roughly 3 minutes.

83
00:08:19,566 --> 00:08:26,749
But both time and temperature are adjustable, fine-tuned based on each batch's starch and moisture levels.

84
00:08:26,749 --> 00:08:32,251
Which are carefully measured using a simple but effective test: how the slices float in the oil.

85
00:08:32,251 --> 00:08:38,716
As the chips fry, steam escapes from inside the slices and the evaporated water is replaced by oil.

86
00:08:38,716 --> 00:08:42,366
That's why oil needs to be constantly replenished during the process.

87
00:08:42,366 --> 00:08:50,797
But Lay's doesn't just pour in oil randomly. They've got a secret formula, a consistent 50/50 blend of fresh oil and used oil.

88
00:08:50,797 --> 00:08:55,617
If the oil is all new, the chips soak up too much of it and end up overly greasy.

89
00:08:55,617 --> 00:08:59,671
If it's all used, they turn too dark and lose that fresh, clean flavor.

90
00:08:59,671 --> 00:09:05,918
When the chips reach that perfect golden color, crispy, aromatic, and just right, they're lifted out of the fryer.

91
00:09:05,918 --> 00:09:11,109
And if you notice everything on the line gently vibrating, that's completely intentional.

92
00:09:11,109 --> 00:09:23,958
Because Lay's Classic chips are ultra thin and fragile, the entire system from conveyors to shoots to transfer points is designed to shake gently, just enough to move the chips forward without cracking them.

93
00:09:23,958 --> 00:09:30,606
If they used regular rotating belts like they do with raw potatoes, all you'd get in the bag would be a pile of crumbs.

94
00:09:30,606 --> 00:09:34,839
And nobody's paying for a bag of potato dust.

95
00:09:34,839 --> 00:09:41,071
[接受质检] Next, the chips pass through a state-of-the-art optical sorter that scans over 400 slices per second.

96
00:09:41,071 --> 00:09:49,205
This machine acts like a hyper-sensitive pair of eyes, constantly on the lookout for brown spots, burnt edges, or any surface defects.

97
00:09:49,205 --> 00:10:01,743
If a chip doesn't meet the standard, the machine responds instantly, firing a pinpoint jet of air, accurate down to the millimeter, to blow that slice right out of the product stream and into a separate reject tray.

98
00:10:01,743 --> 00:10:13,700
Thanks to this lightning-fast inspection step, when you open a bag of Lay's, you're almost guaranteed to find perfectly golden, crispy, intact chips.

99
00:10:13,700 --> 00:10:20,531
Right after leaving the fryer, the golden crispy chips get a light dusting of salt to enhance their basic flavor.

100
00:10:20,531 --> 00:10:27,347
[Seasoning] But the real magic, the signature Lay's taste, happens in the next step: seasoning.

101
00:10:27,347 --> 00:10:35,146
Natural spice blends like chili, black pepper, and herbs are funneled through a dispensing tube inside a giant rotating drum.

102
00:10:35,146 --> 00:10:42,611
As the drum spins, the seasonings are evenly distributed, coating both sides of each chip in a thin, flavorful layer.

103
00:10:42,611 --> 00:10:49,232
Just a few spins in this specialized mixing barrel are enough to ensure every chip is perfectly seasoned from edge to edge.

104
00:10:49,232 --> 00:10:54,291
And since the chips are still hot at this stage, the seasoning sticks like a charm.

105
00:10:54,291 --> 00:10:59,307
At this point, random batches are pulled for quality checks before packaging.

106
00:10:59,307 --> 00:11:07,502
Inspectors test everything from crunch and flavor to color and seasoning coverage, making sure every bite lives up to Lay's standards.

107
00:11:07,502 --> 00:11:16,432
After seasoning, the chips are carried upward by a bucket conveyor to the upper level where a high-speed weighing system with 120 heads gets to work.

108
00:11:16,432 --> 00:11:22,286
It calculates the optimal combination of chips from different trays to hit the exact target weight for each bag.

109
00:11:22,286 --> 00:11:37,162
Whether it's a 1 oz snack size or a 13 oz party pack, every portion is measured down to the chip.

110
00:11:37,162 --> 00:11:33,374
[Packaging] Finally, the chips are sent down to the packaging machines on the ground floor.

111
00:11:33,374 --> 00:11:40,769
Once the bag is filled, the machine flushes it with nitrogen gas, replacing the oxygen to help keep the chips from going stale too quickly.

112
00:11:40,769 --> 00:11:43,455
Then the bag is heat sealed shut.

113
00:11:43,455 --> 00:11:47,761
That nitrogen also creates the signature air pocket in every Lay's bag.

114
00:11:47,761 --> 00:11:53,136
It's not just for show. It acts as a cushion to protect the delicate chips during shipping.

115
00:11:53,136 --> 00:11:58,621
The finished bags are then neatly packed into cardboard boxes and sent off to the palletizing area.

116
00:11:58,621 --> 00:12:06,705
There, the boxes are stacked onto large pallets and tightly wrapped in durable plastic film to keep everything secure and stable during transport.

117
00:12:06,705 --> 00:12:08,586
And then comes the final step.

118
00:12:08,586 --> 00:12:16,722
[出厂] The fully loaded pallets are lifted onto trucks, ready to roll out and deliver crispy golden snacks to Lay's fans all over the world.

119
00:12:16,722 --> 00:12:20,661
And that's how those irresistibly crispy Lay's potato chips are made.

120
00:12:20,661 --> 00:12:24,386
But what about you? Can you stop at just one chip?

121
00:12:24,386 --> 00:12:26,820
Let us know in the comments below.

122
00:12:26,820 --> 00:12:33,147
If you enjoyed this video, don't forget to hit that like button and subscribe so you won't miss any of our upcoming episodes.

123
00:12:33,147 --> 00:12:35,647
And if you've got a fellow Lay's lover in your life, go ahead and share this video with them, too.

